Sometimes, yes. Being silly can signal that the relationship or subject is secure enough to tolerate play.

Humor can do several things at once: lower defenses, show confidence, reveal shared understanding, test whether someone catches the subtext, and make a serious point memorable without flattening it into a lecture. The joke becomes a carrier wave for the serious thing underneath it.

So the useful distinction is:

\text{silliness} \neq \text{lack of seriousness}

Sometimes it is seriousness with enough confidence to move freely.

And of course, context matters. If the other person cannot see the underlying layer, they may only receive the joke. But when both people recognize the frame, play can actually be one of the clearest forms of sincerity.